\begin_layout Section
Wish Specification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This project focuses on developing a RISC-V core specifically designed for
 embedded systems and custom hardware projects.
 The core prioritizes achieving a balanced trade-off between three critical
 factors: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• 
\series bold
Performance
\series default
: The core should deliver efficient processing capabilities.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• 
\series bold
Power Consumption
\series default
: Low power consumption is crucial for battery-powered applications.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• 
\series bold
Area Footprint
\series default
: Minimizing the core's physical size on a chip optimizes cost and resource
 utilization.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The RISC-V core will leverage a Harvard architecture to enable simultaneous
 instruction and data memory accesses.
 This approach enhances overall processing efficiency.
 Additionally, an optimizing folded 6-stage pipeline will be implemented.
 This pipeline maximizes overlap between execution stages and memory accesses,
 reducing stalls and improving performance.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Optional features include Branch Prediction, Instruction Cache, Data Cache,
 Debug Unit and optional Multiplier/Divider Units.
 Parameterized and configurable features include the instruction and data
 interfaces, the branch-prediction-unit configuration, and the cache size,
 associativity, replacement algorithms and multiplier latency.
 Providing the user with trade offs between performance, power, and area
 to optimize the core for the application.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Key Specifications:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
High Performance 32-bit CPU design with Optional Extensions 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Parameterized 32/64bit data
\series default
: The core will be a high-performance 32-bit CPU design with the option
 for parameterized 32/64-bit data handling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Fast and Precise Interrupts
\series default
: The core prioritizes efficient interrupt handling, ensuring timely responses
 to critical events within the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
5-Stage Pipeline
\series default
: This core implements an optimized folded 6-stage pipeline to maximize
 overlap between instruction execution and memory access stages.
 This approach minimizes stalls and improves overall processing efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Memory Protection Support
\series default
: The core will offer memory protection support for enhanced system security.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Optional/Parameterized Caches
\series default
: Instruction and data caches can be optionally integrated to reduce memory
 access latency for performance-critical applications.
 The size, associativity, and replacement algorithms of these caches will
 be configurable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Area and Power Efficiency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The design will prioritizes both minimizing the core's physical footprint
 (area) and reducing its power consumption:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Parameterized Data Width
\series default
: The datapath width can be configured, allowing users to optimize the balance
 between performance and power consumption for their specific application.
 Wider data paths can process more data per cycle but require more hardware
 resources and consume more power.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Clock Gating Logic
\series default
: The core will employ clock gating logic to reduce dynamic power consumption.
 This technique essentially shuts off the clock signal to inactive portions
 of the core, minimizing unnecessary power usage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Small Silicon Footprint
\series default
: The design prioritizes a compact layout to minimize the core's physical
 size on the chip.
 This not only reduces manufacturing costs but also allows for more efficient
 integration with other components on the same die.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Instruction Set Architecture (ISA)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The RISC-V core will support the base integer instruction set architecture
 (ISA) defined by the RISC-V specification.
 This core ISA provides a foundation for efficient execution of various
 workloads.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Basic Integer Instructions
\series default
: The core will support fundamental instructions for arithmetic, logical,
 data transfer, and control flow operations.
 This includes essential instructions like branch, jump, and call instructions
 for program branching and subroutine management.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Optional Branch Prediction
\series default
: A branch prediction algorithm can be optionally integrated to improve
 the efficiency of branch instructions.
 Branch prediction attempts to forecast the outcome of a conditional branch
 instruction, potentially reducing pipeline stalls and improving overall
 performance.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Custom ISA Extension Support (Optional)
\series default
: The design may offer the flexibility to incorporate user-defined RISC-V
 instruction extensions.
 These extensions can be tailored to specific application requirements,
 potentially accelerating performance for specialized tasks.
 However, the decision to include custom extensions requires careful considerati
on of development complexity and potential compatibility limitations.
\end_layout

